{0 oyster-odoc-reader}

Read odoc documentation into an OysterMark vault: one note per odoc page.

{1 Where it enters}

By the time odoc writes a [.odocl] file it has parsed the doc comments,
resolved every cross-reference, rendered the signatures and assigned an anchor
to each declaration. The file itself is an internal OCaml [Marshal] value. The
reader therefore asks the [odoc] executable on [PATH] to consume it and emit
embeddable JSON, then translates the JSON metadata and HTML fragments. The
installed command may be used across OCaml switches because only the active
[odoc] process touches its internal representation.

Run the reader in the same active environment as [dune build @doc]. The
cross-process contract is the textual output of
[odoc html-generate --as-json], whose fields and HTML shapes used here have
reader fixtures.

Entry point: {!Oyster_odoc_reader.Notes.of_odocl}.

{1 How each construct is spelled}

{ul
{- {b Signatures} stay in a fenced code block. A fence's content is literal, so
   it cannot hold a link; the cross-references odoc resolved inside the
   signature are collected and emitted as wikilinks on a [Refs:] line beneath
   the fence. The edges reach the vault graph; their position within the
   signature does not survive.}
{- {b Documented members} — a record field or a constructor with its own anchor
   and prose — keep their code line inside that same fence. No anchor can
   attach to a line inside a code block, so the anchor and the prose follow the
   fence as a keyed item.}
{- {b Tags} ([@param], [@raise], [@since]) arrive as a description whose key
   splits into a tag name and an optional argument, which is the label chain a
   keyed node wants: [- parameter: `x`: ...].}
{- {b Expansions} each become a note of their own, whatever odoc's inline
   preference was, and the fence that held one collapses to [sig ... end].}
}

{1 Addressing}

Notes mirror odoc's own page tree — a directory per module, carrying odoc's
disambiguating [kind-] prefix — so a module and a module type of the same name
do not collide. See {!Oyster_odoc_reader.Address}.

Anchor identifiers admit only \[A-Za-z0-9_-\]. A ['.'] would read as a djot
class separator, so odoc's field and constructor anchors ([type-t.field]) are
rewritten and the original kept in an [odoc-anchor] key.

{1 Known gaps}

{ul
{- The per-token syntax classes odoc attaches inside a signature
   ([Source.Tag]) are lost when the signature flattens into a fence.}
{- [source_anchor], odoc's link from an item back to its source code, is not
   emitted.}
{- A page's frontmatter — [short_title], [children_order], [toc_status],
   [order_category] — is not carried into YAML frontmatter.}
}
